{"kind":"llm","key":"llm-cdad04e4c8c6a2b390b5911b65c98eaf605656f0366e66a891943b3977e13eb1","recordedAt":"2026-11-10T08:14:19.261Z","liveMs":20055,"request":{"model":"gpt-5-mini-<date>","messages":[{"role":"user","content":"You are the NIETE Teaching Assistant, a warm teacher coach. Below is a REAL coaching session. Produce a short \"commitment card\" the teacher receives on WhatsApp after our reflective conversation.\n\nWRITE ALL THREE TEXT FIELDS (commitment, action, lesson_label) IN ENGLISH — this teacher's lesson and our whole conversation were in English. Natural, warm, native English.\n\nGENDER-NEUTRAL — teachers are BOTH men and women. NEVER use gendered second-person verb forms. English is gender-neutral; address as \"you\".\n\nCODE-SWITCH LIKE A REAL TEACHER TEXTS. Pedagogical / technical / subject-matter terms MUST appear in ENGLISH (Latin letters) inline — NEVER translate them into English and NEVER transliterate them into English script. Teachers SAY these in English even mid-sentence: open-ended questions, conjunction, paragraph, pair reading, Think-Pair-Share, wait time, objective, model, fractions, percentage, group work, peer feedback.\nKeep terms natural; no transliteration needed.\n\nPLAIN LANGUAGE — the teacher must understand every word. Do NOT use coach-jargon she wouldn't say herself: \"scaffolding\", \"extension\", \"differentiation\", \"formative assessment\", \"higher-order thinking\", \"metacognition\", \"gradual release\". Describe the concrete move in plain words instead (e.g. instead of \"scaffolding\", write \"break it into small steps\"; instead of \"an extension\", write \"a harder task for the ones who finish early\").\n\nThe card has TWO parts:\n1. \"commitment\" — a single warm sentence (max ~18 words) in the teacher's OWN spirit. Reflect back what SHE values, drawn from her Q3 answer (her forward-looking reflection). Address her as \"you\"/\"we\". No honorifics, no name inside it.\n2. \"action\" — ONE specific, concrete thing to try in her NEXT class. It MUST be rooted in THIS exact lesson AND fuse her own value (from her Q3 answer + strengths) with THE TARGET below. Phrase it as an implementation intention anchored to next class (\"Next class, when [trigger], [do X]\") — but respect the gender-neutral rule above (imperative, not a gendered \"you will\"). Max ~32 words. Vivid and classroom-specific — name the actual materials/concept from THIS lesson. NOT generic.\n\nAlso return \"highlights\": an array of 2–4 short English keyword phrases that appear verbatim in \"action\" (concrete nouns) to visually emphasise. And \"lesson_label\": a 2–4 word English subject·topic label.\n\nSession (framework: FICO):\nTHE TARGET (fixed — do NOT choose a different area): indicator C3 \"Effective Feedback\". Why it is the next step: Effective feedback was absent (score 0): teacher mostly repeated correct answers or used praise but did not tell students what to do next. Targeting C3 will immediately improve learning gains as students will correct errors and know the next steps. The scorer's suggested move: \"After a student's answer, say: 'Good — you named X. To be complete, add Y.' Then ask the student to repeat the fixed phrasing.\". Your \"action\" is about THIS indicator only — ONE move, not a list of moves.\n\n- Her strengths: Clear models and real-world analogy\n- Growth areas: Effective Feedback — Feedback mostly accepted answers or used praise; there were no explicit corrective or next-step statements (e.g., teacher repeats correct term or says 'شاباش' but does not tell students how to improve). Strategy: After a student answer, use a three-part micro-feedback: 'You named X; to be complete, add Y; please repeat.' (This aligns with the observer's 'try this <reltime>' script.) || Wait-Time & Inclusion for Quieter Students — Some students are quiet and the teacher's quick pacing means many turns are chorus responses or immediate counts rather than reasoned answers. Strategy: Deliberately pause for 5–10 seconds after asking a conceptual question; silently count in your head to signal wait-time. || Differentiation & Productive Struggle — All students received the same task and support; no easier starter tasks or extension tasks were offered. Strategy: Prepare two practice tiers (identification …(+763)"}],"response_format":{"type":"json_object"}},"value":{"id":"gen-1794298439-oRbReupGIBEy6305dfM7","object":"chat.completion","created":1794298439,"model":"openai/gpt-5-mini","provider":"OpenAI","system_fingerprint":null,"service_tier":"default","choices":[{"index":0,"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop","native_finish_reason":"completed","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"commitment\":\"We’ll keep using clear models and slow the pace so quieter students can think and answer.\",\"action\":\"Next class, when a student says only '8 below' in the 8-part model, pause and say: 'You named 8; to be complete, add the numerator—please repeat \\\"numerator over denominator\\\".'\",\"lesson_label\":\"Math: Fractions\",\"highlights\":[\"8-part model\",\"You named 8\",\"numerator over denominator\"]}","refusal":null,"reasoning":"**Crafting commitment sentences**\n\nI need to create a commitment sentence that's warm and reflects the user's values, particularly about pacing and giving quieter students extra time. Something like, \"We'll use clear models and slow the pace so quieter students can think and answer,\" which comes to 16 words. It's important to maintain that warm tone while ensuring it's gender-neutral. I want to make sure it captures the teacher's spirit and aligns with their feedback from Q3.**Implementing feedback intention**\n\nI need to set up an implementation intention for the next class focused on Effective Feedback, specifically using a three-part micro-feedback approach. The action will be anchored to the lesson on fractions. So, when a student answers a fraction question, I'll pause and say: “Good — you named the denominator 8; to be complete, add the numerator (what's above); please repeat: numerator over denominator.” I want to keep it direct and non-gendered, fitting within the 32-word limit while including the essential feedback structure.**Finalizing feedback implementation**\n\nI need to implement this feedback for the next class. When a student names only the denominator, like saying \"8 below,\" I'll pause and say: \"You named 8; to be complete, add the numerator; please repeat 'numerator over denominator.'\" I want to keep this concise, aiming for about 32 words total. I'll double-check the word count to make sure it's within the limit and adjusts as needed. Clarity is key for effective feedback!**Refining feedback statement**\n\nI need to create a concise feedback statement for the next class. It should say something like, \"Next class, when a student says '8 below' during the fractions model, pause and say: 'You named the denominator 8; to be complete, add the numerator; please repeat \"numerator over denominator.\"'” I counted the words, and it comes out to 33, which is a bit over 32. So, I should tweak it slightly to stay within the suggested limit. Clarity while being concise is crucial!**Preparing the JSON output**\n\nI need to draft a concise feedback statement for the next class. Let’s refine the action statement to be about \"when a student says '8 below' in the 8-part model\" and keep it around 28 words. Now, I'll gather the essential phrases: \"8-part model,\" \"You named 8,\" and \"numerator over denominator.\" For the lesson label, I can use \"Math: Fractions.\" I'll ensure the commitment is limited to 18 words, focusing on clear communication for quieter students. Now I’ll prepare the final JSON output.","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.summary","summary":"**Crafting commitment sentences**\n\nI need to create a commitment sentence that's warm and reflects the user's values, particularly about pacing and giving quieter students extra time. Something like, \"We'll use clear models and sl